.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="van&#x27;t Hoff (1884) classified reactions by the order n of their rate law -d[A]/dt = k[A]^n. The three textbook cases -- ZeroOrder, FirstOrder, and SecondOrder -- all started from the same initial concentration and with rate constants chosen to give the same half-life, so the different curvature (linear, exponential, hyperbolic) is the only thing distinguishing them. First order is the only one whose half-life doesn&#x27;t depend on the starting concentration; the plot below marks all three half-lives to make that concrete.">

